{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:InformationTheory.CompositeCrossingParity","@type":"LeanModule","name":"InformationTheory.CompositeCrossingParity","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Nat.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.NormNum"],"declarations":13,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Information.iodine_odd","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Information.iodine_odd","kind":"theorem","line":100,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/information-theory/composite-crossing-parity.lean#L100-L102","doc":"**Iodine** (Z = 53, N = 74): odd crossing number. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Information.iron_even","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Information.iron_even","kind":"theorem","line":96,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/information-theory/composite-crossing-parity.lean#L96-L98","doc":"**Iron** (Z = 26, N = 30): even crossing number. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Information.arsenic_odd","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Information.arsenic_odd","kind":"theorem","line":92,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/information-theory/composite-crossing-parity.lean#L92-L94","doc":"**Arsenic** (Z = 33, N = 42): odd crossing number. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Information.nitrogen_odd","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Information.nitrogen_odd","kind":"theorem","line":88,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/information-theory/composite-crossing-parity.lean#L88-L90","doc":"**Nitrogen** (Z = 7, N = 7): odd crossing number. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Information.helium_even","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Information.helium_even","kind":"theorem","line":84,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/information-theory/composite-crossing-parity.lean#L84-L86","doc":"**Helium has even crossing number** (Z = 2 is even). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Information.helium_crossing","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Information.helium_crossing","kind":"theorem","line":79,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/information-theory/composite-crossing-parity.lean#L79-L82","doc":"**Helium** (Z = 2, N = 2): crossing number n_K = A²+7A+5Z = 16+28+10 = 54,\nwhich is even. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Information.hydrogen_odd","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Information.hydrogen_odd","kind":"theorem","line":75,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/information-theory/composite-crossing-parity.lean#L75-L77","doc":"**Hydrogen has odd crossing number** (Z = 1 is odd). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Information.hydrogen_crossing","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Information.hydrogen_crossing","kind":"theorem","line":71,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/information-theory/composite-crossing-parity.lean#L71-L73","doc":"**Hydrogen** (Z = 1, N = 0): crossing number n_K = 13, which is odd. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Information.odd_Z_odd_crossing","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Information.odd_Z_odd_crossing","kind":"theorem","line":66,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/information-theory/composite-crossing-parity.lean#L66-L69","doc":"Atoms with odd atomic number have odd crossing number. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Information.even_Z_even_crossing","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Information.even_Z_even_crossing","kind":"theorem","line":61,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/information-theory/composite-crossing-parity.lean#L61-L64","doc":"Atoms with even atomic number have even crossing number. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Information.crossing_parity_eq_Z","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Information.crossing_parity_eq_Z","kind":"theorem","line":39,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/information-theory/composite-crossing-parity.lean#L39-L59","doc":"**Main theorem**: the composite crossing number has the same parity\nas the atomic number Z.\n\nn_K(A(Z,N)) ≡ Z  (mod 2)\n\nProof: n_K = A² + 7A + 5Z.  Since A² + 7A = A(A+7) and\nA(A+7) ≡ A(A+1) + 6A ≡ 0 + 0 ≡ 0 (mod 2) (because A(A+1) is the\nproduct of consecutive integers, always even, and 6A is always even),\nwe get n_K ≡ 5Z ≡ Z (mod 2). ","formalizes":[{"@id":"block:conj:composite-crossing-parity","label":"conj:composite-crossing-parity","md":"quantum-observable-universe/information-theory/composite-crossing-parity.md"}],"signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Information.crossingBudget","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Information.crossingBudget","kind":"theorem","line":26,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/information-theory/composite-crossing-parity.lean#L26-L37","doc":"The crossing budget decomposes into four contributions. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Information.compositeCrossingNumber","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Information.compositeCrossingNumber","kind":"def","line":15,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/information-theory/composite-crossing-parity.lean#L15-L24","doc":"**Composite crossing number** of an atom A(Z, N) with mass number A = Z + N.\nn_K = A² + 7A + 5Z from the crossing budget:\n- 8A (nucleon internal: 6 Borromean + 2 Hopf per nucleon)\n- A(A-1) (inter-nucleon Hopf links: 2 per pair × C(A,2))\n- 3Z (electron trefoils)\n- 2Z (electron-nucleus Hopf binding)\nLabel: conj:composite-crossing-parity ","signatureChunk":0}]}